{"id": "grounded-no-citations", "criterion": "grounded", "label": 2, "why": "Claims all match the findings but none carries a citation; the rubric caps this at 2.", "material": {"topic": "how PostgreSQL prevents transaction ID wraparound", "expect": "32-bit transaction IDs, freezing old tuples, autovacuum and anti-wraparound vacuum, the relevant settings, and what happens if it is ignored.", "findings": [{"question": "Why is wraparound a risk?", "answer": "Transaction IDs are 32-bit and compared modulo 2^32, so after about two billion transactions old rows would appear to be in the future and become invisible.", "sources": ["PostgreSQL docs: Routine Vacuuming"], "confidence": 0.9, "reviewer_note": ""}, {"question": "How does freezing prevent it?", "answer": "VACUUM marks sufficiently old tuples as frozen, removing them from XID comparison; autovacuum forces an anti-wraparound vacuum when a table's relfrozenxid age exceeds autovacuum_freeze_max_age (200 million by default).", "sources": ["PostgreSQL docs: Routine Vacuuming", "PostgreSQL docs: autovacuum settings"], "confidence": 0.9, "reviewer_note": ""}, {"question": "What happens if vacuum falls behind?", "answer": "Near the limit PostgreSQL warns, then refuses to assign new transaction IDs until a VACUUM advances the oldest XID; vacuum_failsafe_age (1.6 billion) makes vacuum skip non-essential work to catch up.", "sources": ["PostgreSQL docs: Routine Vacuuming"], "confidence": 0.85, "reviewer_note": ""}], "briefing": "PostgreSQL prevents wraparound by freezing old rows. XIDs are 32-bit and compared modulo 2^32, so unfrozen rows older than about two billion transactions would become invisible. Autovacuum forces a freezing vacuum when a table's relfrozenxid age passes 200 million. If vacuum falls behind, the server refuses new transaction IDs until it catches up.\n\nSources\n[1] PostgreSQL docs: Routine Vacuuming - https://example.org/1\n[2] PostgreSQL docs: Routine Vacuuming - https://example.org/2\n[3] PostgreSQL docs: autovacuum settings - https://example.org/3\n[4] PostgreSQL docs: Routine Vacuuming - https://example.org/4"}}
{"id": "grounded-invented-figures", "criterion": "grounded", "label": 1, "why": "COP 3.5 at -25 C and 90% of Nordic homes appear in no finding, yet carry citations.", "material": {"topic": "field performance of air-source heat pumps in cold climates, evidence since 2020 only", "expect": "Field-measured efficiency (COP) at low temperatures, how backup heat is used, and studies from 2020 onward.", "findings": [{"question": "What seasonal efficiency do cold-climate heat pumps reach in the field?", "answer": "Field studies since 2020 in Canada and the northern US report seasonal COPs of about 2.5-3.0 for cold-climate models.", "sources": ["NEEP cold-climate field study 2022", "NRCan field monitoring report"], "confidence": 0.8, "reviewer_note": ""}, {"question": "What is the COP near -15 C?", "answer": "One study measured a COP of about 2.0 at -15 C; another measured about 1.5 at the same temperature.", "sources": ["Minnesota CARD field study", "Alaska heat pump monitoring"], "confidence": 0.45, "reviewer_note": "Sources disagree by a third; different models and installs."}, {"question": "How much backup heat is used?", "answer": "Backup resistance or fossil heat supplied 5-20% of seasonal heat in monitored homes, mostly during the coldest days.", "sources": ["NEEP cold-climate field study 2022"], "confidence": 0.75, "reviewer_note": ""}], "briefing": "Cold-climate heat pumps reach a COP of 3.5 even at -25 C [1] and now heat 90% of Nordic homes [2]. Seasonal COPs of 2.5-3.0 are typical [1][2], and backup heat covers 5-20% of the season [5].\n\nSources\n[1] NEEP cold-climate field study 2022 - https://example.org/1\n[2] NRCan field monitoring report - https://example.org/2\n[3] Minnesota CARD field study - https://example.org/3\n[4] Alaska heat pump monitoring - https://example.org/4\n[5] NEEP cold-climate field study 2022 - https://example.org/5"}}
{"id": "grounded-minor-detail", "criterion": "grounded", "label": 3, "why": "All substantive claims supported and cited; 'Trip.com is a large travel company' is an uncited background detail.", "material": {"topic": "does remote work reduce employee productivity", "expect": "Shows the evidence is mixed: distinguishes fully remote from hybrid, cites studies on both sides, and does not declare a single verdict.", "findings": [{"question": "What does randomized evidence say about hybrid work?", "answer": "A large randomized trial at Trip.com found two days a week at home had no effect on performance reviews or promotions and cut quitting by a third.", "sources": ["Nature 2024 hybrid work RCT"], "confidence": 0.85, "reviewer_note": ""}, {"question": "What about fully remote work?", "answer": "Two studies of data-entry and call-centre work found fully remote staff 10-20% less productive.", "sources": ["NBER fully remote data-entry study", "Call-centre field experiment"], "confidence": 0.6, "reviewer_note": "Only two studies, both narrow task types; may not generalise to knowledge work."}, {"question": "How do workers rate their own productivity at home?", "answer": "Most surveyed workers say they are as productive or more productive at home.", "sources": ["WFH Research survey"], "confidence": 0.4, "reviewer_note": "Self-reported; not a productivity measure."}], "briefing": "Hybrid work does not reduce productivity: a randomized trial found two home days a week had no effect on performance and cut quitting by a third [1]. Trip.com is a large online travel company. Two studies of data-entry and call-centre work found fully remote staff 10-20% less productive [2][3], and surveys where workers rate themselves are self-reported [4].\n\nSources\n[1] Nature 2024 hybrid work RCT - https://example.org/1\n[2] NBER fully remote data-entry study - https://example.org/2\n[3] Call-centre field experiment - https://example.org/3\n[4] WFH Research survey - https://example.org/4"}}
{"id": "grounded-miscited-haleu", "criterion": "grounded", "label": 2, "why": "The HALEU claim cites the UAMPS cancellation announcement [1] instead of the DOE page [4].", "material": {"topic": "what limits deployment of small modular reactors in the US", "expect": "Cost and first-of-a-kind risk, NRC licensing, fuel supply (HALEU), and named projects including at least one that was cancelled or delayed.", "findings": [{"question": "What happened to NuScale's first project?", "answer": "NuScale and UAMPS cancelled the Carbon Free Power Project in 2023 after estimated power costs rose to about $89/MWh.", "sources": ["UAMPS announcement", "Utility Dive coverage"], "confidence": 0.85, "reviewer_note": ""}, {"question": "How long does NRC licensing take?", "answer": "Design certification plus a construction permit has historically taken several years; recent reforms set shorter review targets.", "sources": ["NRC licensing overview"], "confidence": 0.75, "reviewer_note": ""}, {"question": "Is fuel supply a constraint?", "answer": "Many advanced designs need HALEU, and US commercial production is at demonstration scale.", "sources": ["DOE HALEU program page"], "confidence": 0.8, "reviewer_note": ""}], "briefing": "Cost is the main limit: NuScale's first project was cancelled in 2023 at about $89/MWh [1][2]. Licensing has taken several years [3]. Many designs need HALEU, which the US produces only at demonstration scale [1].\n\nSources\n[1] UAMPS announcement - https://example.org/1\n[2] Utility Dive coverage - https://example.org/2\n[3] NRC licensing overview - https://example.org/3\n[4] DOE HALEU program page - https://example.org/4"}}
{"id": "grounded-weak-attributed", "criterion": "grounded", "label": 4, "why": "Every claim is cited to its own finding, including the weak warranty claim, which is attributed to the seller.", "material": {"topic": "long-term outdoor reliability of perovskite solar cells over ten years or more", "expect": "Says plainly that decade-long field data barely exists, reports what shorter field and accelerated tests show, and does not overstate durability.", "findings": [{"question": "What do accelerated ageing tests show for perovskite modules?", "answer": "Leading lab and pilot modules now pass IEC damp-heat and thermal-cycling tests, with some retaining 80% of output after 1,000-2,000 hours of damp heat.", "sources": ["NREL perovskite durability review", "Joule accelerated ageing study"], "confidence": 0.8, "reviewer_note": ""}, {"question": "How long is the longest outdoor field record?", "answer": "Published outdoor records span about two to three years, with degradation rates that vary widely between groups.", "sources": ["Nature Energy outdoor testing paper"], "confidence": 0.7, "reviewer_note": ""}, {"question": "Do any manufacturers guarantee long lifetimes?", "answer": "One startup announced a 25-year performance warranty for its tandem modules.", "sources": ["Company press release"], "confidence": 0.3, "reviewer_note": "Single press release from the seller; no field data behind the warranty."}], "briefing": "Decade-long field data does not exist: outdoor records cover two to three years [3]. Leading modules keep 80% of output after 1,000-2,000 hours of damp heat in accelerated tests [1][2]. One startup offers a 25-year warranty, according to its own press release [4].\n\nSources\n[1] NREL perovskite durability review - https://example.org/1\n[2] Joule accelerated ageing study - https://example.org/2\n[3] Nature Energy outdoor testing paper - https://example.org/3\n[4] Company press release - https://example.org/4"}}
{"id": "covers-history-not-field", "criterion": "covers", "label": 1, "why": "Topic asks for field evidence since 2020; the briefing is mostly history and pre-2020 lab tests.", "material": {"topic": "field performance of air-source heat pumps in cold climates, evidence since 2020 only", "expect": "Field-measured efficiency (COP) at low temperatures, how backup heat is used, and studies from 2020 onward.", "findings": [{"question": "What seasonal efficiency do cold-climate heat pumps reach in the field?", "answer": "Field studies since 2020 in Canada and the northern US report seasonal COPs of about 2.5-3.0 for cold-climate models.", "sources": ["NEEP cold-climate field study 2022", "NRCan field monitoring report"], "confidence": 0.8, "reviewer_note": ""}, {"question": "What is the COP near -15 C?", "answer": "One study measured a COP of about 2.0 at -15 C; another measured about 1.5 at the same temperature.", "sources": ["Minnesota CARD field study", "Alaska heat pump monitoring"], "confidence": 0.45, "reviewer_note": "Sources disagree by a third; different models and installs."}, {"question": "How much backup heat is used?", "answer": "Backup resistance or fossil heat supplied 5-20% of seasonal heat in monitored homes, mostly during the coldest days.", "sources": ["NEEP cold-climate field study 2022"], "confidence": 0.75, "reviewer_note": ""}], "briefing": "Heat pumps were invented in the nineteenth century and spread through Japan and Europe from the 1970s. Early cold-climate models struggled below freezing, and lab tests in the early 2010s found sharp efficiency losses. Today they reach seasonal COPs of 2.5-3.0 [1][2].\n\nSources\n[1] NEEP cold-climate field study 2022 - https://example.org/1\n[2] NRCan field monitoring report - https://example.org/2\n[3] Minnesota CARD field study - https://example.org/3\n[4] Alaska heat pump monitoring - https://example.org/4\n[5] NEEP cold-climate field study 2022 - https://example.org/5"}}
{"id": "covers-heatpump-full", "criterion": "covers", "label": 4, "why": "Field COP, low-temperature performance including the disagreement, backup heat, all from 2020 onward.", "material": {"topic": "field performance of air-source heat pumps in cold climates, evidence since 2020 only", "expect": "Field-measured efficiency (COP) at low temperatures, how backup heat is used, and studies from 2020 onward.", "findings": [{"question": "What seasonal efficiency do cold-climate heat pumps reach in the field?", "answer": "Field studies since 2020 in Canada and the northern US report seasonal COPs of about 2.5-3.0 for cold-climate models.", "sources": ["NEEP cold-climate field study 2022", "NRCan field monitoring report"], "confidence": 0.8, "reviewer_note": ""}, {"question": "What is the COP near -15 C?", "answer": "One study measured a COP of about 2.0 at -15 C; another measured about 1.5 at the same temperature.", "sources": ["Minnesota CARD field study", "Alaska heat pump monitoring"], "confidence": 0.45, "reviewer_note": "Sources disagree by a third; different models and installs."}, {"question": "How much backup heat is used?", "answer": "Backup resistance or fossil heat supplied 5-20% of seasonal heat in monitored homes, mostly during the coldest days.", "sources": ["NEEP cold-climate field study 2022"], "confidence": 0.75, "reviewer_note": ""}], "briefing": "Field studies since 2020 show cold-climate heat pumps reaching seasonal COPs of 2.5-3.0 [1][2]. At -15 C measured COPs range from about 1.5 to 2.0, and the two studies disagree [3][4]. Backup heat supplies 5-20% of seasonal heat, mostly on the coldest days [5].\n\nSources\n[1] NEEP cold-climate field study 2022 - https://example.org/1\n[2] NRCan field monitoring report - https://example.org/2\n[3] Minnesota CARD field study - https://example.org/3\n[4] Alaska heat pump monitoring - https://example.org/4\n[5] NEEP cold-climate field study 2022 - https://example.org/5"}}
{"id": "covers-postgres-full", "criterion": "covers", "label": 4, "why": "32-bit XIDs, freezing, autovacuum and its setting, and what happens if ignored.", "material": {"topic": "how PostgreSQL prevents transaction ID wraparound", "expect": "32-bit transaction IDs, freezing old tuples, autovacuum and anti-wraparound vacuum, the relevant settings, and what happens if it is ignored.", "findings": [{"question": "Why is wraparound a risk?", "answer": "Transaction IDs are 32-bit and compared modulo 2^32, so after about two billion transactions old rows would appear to be in the future and become invisible.", "sources": ["PostgreSQL docs: Routine Vacuuming"], "confidence": 0.9, "reviewer_note": ""}, {"question": "How does freezing prevent it?", "answer": "VACUUM marks sufficiently old tuples as frozen, removing them from XID comparison; autovacuum forces an anti-wraparound vacuum when a table's relfrozenxid age exceeds autovacuum_freeze_max_age (200 million by default).", "sources": ["PostgreSQL docs: Routine Vacuuming", "PostgreSQL docs: autovacuum settings"], "confidence": 0.9, "reviewer_note": ""}, {"question": "What happens if vacuum falls behind?", "answer": "Near the limit PostgreSQL warns, then refuses to assign new transaction IDs until a VACUUM advances the oldest XID; vacuum_failsafe_age (1.6 billion) makes vacuum skip non-essential work to catch up.", "sources": ["PostgreSQL docs: Routine Vacuuming"], "confidence": 0.85, "reviewer_note": ""}], "briefing": "PostgreSQL prevents wraparound by freezing old rows; XIDs are 32-bit and compared modulo 2^32 [1]. Autovacuum forces a freezing vacuum once a table's relfrozenxid age passes autovacuum_freeze_max_age, 200 million by default [2][3]. If vacuum falls behind, the server stops assigning transaction IDs until it catches up, and vacuum_failsafe_age (1.6 billion) makes vacuum skip non-essential work [4].\n\nSources\n[1] PostgreSQL docs: Routine Vacuuming - https://example.org/1\n[2] PostgreSQL docs: Routine Vacuuming - https://example.org/2\n[3] PostgreSQL docs: autovacuum settings - https://example.org/3\n[4] PostgreSQL docs: Routine Vacuuming - https://example.org/4"}}
{"id": "covers-postgres-partial", "criterion": "covers", "label": 2, "why": "Explains the cause and freezing but omits the settings and what happens if vacuum falls behind.", "material": {"topic": "how PostgreSQL prevents transaction ID wraparound", "expect": "32-bit transaction IDs, freezing old tuples, autovacuum and anti-wraparound vacuum, the relevant settings, and what happens if it is ignored.", "findings": [{"question": "Why is wraparound a risk?", "answer": "Transaction IDs are 32-bit and compared modulo 2^32, so after about two billion transactions old rows would appear to be in the future and become invisible.", "sources": ["PostgreSQL docs: Routine Vacuuming"], "confidence": 0.9, "reviewer_note": ""}, {"question": "How does freezing prevent it?", "answer": "VACUUM marks sufficiently old tuples as frozen, removing them from XID comparison; autovacuum forces an anti-wraparound vacuum when a table's relfrozenxid age exceeds autovacuum_freeze_max_age (200 million by default).", "sources": ["PostgreSQL docs: Routine Vacuuming", "PostgreSQL docs: autovacuum settings"], "confidence": 0.9, "reviewer_note": ""}, {"question": "What happens if vacuum falls behind?", "answer": "Near the limit PostgreSQL warns, then refuses to assign new transaction IDs until a VACUUM advances the oldest XID; vacuum_failsafe_age (1.6 billion) makes vacuum skip non-essential work to catch up.", "sources": ["PostgreSQL docs: Routine Vacuuming"], "confidence": 0.85, "reviewer_note": ""}], "briefing": "Transaction IDs are 32-bit and compared modulo 2^32, so very old rows would look like they are from the future [1]. VACUUM prevents this by freezing old rows [2].\n\nSources\n[1] PostgreSQL docs: Routine Vacuuming - https://example.org/1\n[2] PostgreSQL docs: Routine Vacuuming - https://example.org/2\n[3] PostgreSQL docs: autovacuum settings - https://example.org/3\n[4] PostgreSQL docs: Routine Vacuuming - https://example.org/4"}}
{"id": "covers-remote-thin", "criterion": "covers", "label": 3, "why": "Distinguishes hybrid from fully remote and avoids a single verdict, but the fully-remote side rests on one sentence.", "material": {"topic": "does remote work reduce employee productivity", "expect": "Shows the evidence is mixed: distinguishes fully remote from hybrid, cites studies on both sides, and does not declare a single verdict.", "findings": [{"question": "What does randomized evidence say about hybrid work?", "answer": "A large randomized trial at Trip.com found two days a week at home had no effect on performance reviews or promotions and cut quitting by a third.", "sources": ["Nature 2024 hybrid work RCT"], "confidence": 0.85, "reviewer_note": ""}, {"question": "What about fully remote work?", "answer": "Two studies of data-entry and call-centre work found fully remote staff 10-20% less productive.", "sources": ["NBER fully remote data-entry study", "Call-centre field experiment"], "confidence": 0.6, "reviewer_note": "Only two studies, both narrow task types; may not generalise to knowledge work."}, {"question": "How do workers rate their own productivity at home?", "answer": "Most surveyed workers say they are as productive or more productive at home.", "sources": ["WFH Research survey"], "confidence": 0.4, "reviewer_note": "Self-reported; not a productivity measure."}], "briefing": "The answer depends on the arrangement. Hybrid work does not reduce productivity: a large randomized trial found no effect on performance [1]. Fully remote work may cost some productivity [2].\n\nSources\n[1] Nature 2024 hybrid work RCT - https://example.org/1\n[2] NBER fully remote data-entry study - https://example.org/2\n[3] Call-centre field experiment - https://example.org/3\n[4] WFH Research survey - https://example.org/4"}}
{"id": "covers-smr-thin-licensing", "criterion": "covers", "label": 3, "why": "Cost, a cancelled project and HALEU are covered; licensing gets only a passing mention.", "material": {"topic": "what limits deployment of small modular reactors in the US", "expect": "Cost and first-of-a-kind risk, NRC licensing, fuel supply (HALEU), and named projects including at least one that was cancelled or delayed.", "findings": [{"question": "What happened to NuScale's first project?", "answer": "NuScale and UAMPS cancelled the Carbon Free Power Project in 2023 after estimated power costs rose to about $89/MWh.", "sources": ["UAMPS announcement", "Utility Dive coverage"], "confidence": 0.85, "reviewer_note": ""}, {"question": "How long does NRC licensing take?", "answer": "Design certification plus a construction permit has historically taken several years; recent reforms set shorter review targets.", "sources": ["NRC licensing overview"], "confidence": 0.75, "reviewer_note": ""}, {"question": "Is fuel supply a constraint?", "answer": "Many advanced designs need HALEU, and US commercial production is at demonstration scale.", "sources": ["DOE HALEU program page"], "confidence": 0.8, "reviewer_note": ""}], "briefing": "Cost is the main limit: NuScale's first project was cancelled in 2023 when power costs rose to about $89/MWh [1][2]. Fuel is the next: many designs need HALEU, produced in the US only at demonstration scale [4]. Licensing also takes time [3].\n\nSources\n[1] UAMPS announcement - https://example.org/1\n[2] Utility Dive coverage - https://example.org/2\n[3] NRC licensing overview - https://example.org/3\n[4] DOE HALEU program page - https://example.org/4"}}
